Short version: both are excellent, and the right pick depends mostly on what your team already knows and what kind of workload you're serving.

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Stack([cmp])
cmp = Comparison("Backend language choice", [py, js], true)
py = {name:"Python", attributes:["Very readable — fastest to onboard","Sync-first; async works but is opt-in","Unbeatable for data, ML and AI workloads","Django / FastAPI are mature and batteries-included","Weaker at massive concurrent I/O","Enormous hiring pool"]}
js = {name:"JavaScript / Node.js", attributes:["One language front to back","Async I/O is the native model","Huge npm ecosystem, uneven quality","Express / Nest / Fastify — strong for APIs and websockets","CPU-heavy work blocks the event loop","Reuse your existing frontend hires"]}
:::

**My recommendation:** pick **Python** if the backend touches data, ML, analytics, or AI, or if you value clarity and a big hiring pool — FastAPI or Django will get you there fast. Pick **Node.js** if you're building realtime APIs, websockets, or a thin BFF layer, and especially if your team already writes JavaScript and you want one language everywhere.

The tiebreaker that usually matters more than benchmarks: whichever one your team can maintain confidently. Both handle typical web traffic fine — the language is rarely the bottleneck.

