Both are mature, battle-tested relational databases, and for most workloads the choice comes down to how much you value strictness and feature depth versus simplicity and raw speed on simple reads.

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Stack([cmp])
cmp = Comparison("PostgreSQL vs MySQL", [pg, my])
pg = {name: "PostgreSQL", attributes: ["Object-relational, highly extensible", "Closest to full SQL standard", "MVCC + strict ACID, no caveats", "Rich types: JSONB, arrays, ranges, geospatial (PostGIS)", "Advanced indexes: GIN, GiST, BRIN, partial, expression", "Streaming + logical replication, sync replicas", "Permissive PostgreSQL licence (BSD-like)", "Sweet spot: complex queries, analytics, data integrity"]}
my = {name: "MySQL", attributes: ["Relational, simpler and more predictable", "Very fast on simple read-heavy workloads", "ACID with InnoDB (MyISAM is not transactional)", "JSON type, but weaker indexing of it", "Basic B-tree / fulltext indexes", "Async + semi-sync replication, group replication", "GPLv2 or commercial (Oracle)", "Sweet spot: LAMP stacks, high-volume CRUD, easy hosting"]}
:::

**The short answer:** pick PostgreSQL for a new project unless you have a specific reason not to. It handles complexity better — window functions, CTEs, JSONB, geospatial, and constraints that actually enforce your business rules — and its extension ecosystem means you often don't need a second database.

**Pick MySQL when** your workload is mostly simple key-value-ish CRUD at very high read volume, your team already knows it, or you're deploying into an environment where MySQL is the path of least resistance (shared hosting, certain managed clouds, WordPress/LAMP). Note that in practice this choice is usually "MySQL or MariaDB", and MariaDB is often the better community-governed option in that family.

One caveat that matters more than the benchmark wars: a well-indexed, well-modelled schema in either engine will beat a badly modelled one in the other, every time.

